course: dictionary
lesson: 6423
title: Quiet words with loud meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words describe how someone speaks, not just what they say. Muttering is speech that is quiet and hard to hear, often because the speaker is grumbling or talking to themselves. «He was muttering to himself» tells you two things: he was talking, and he was not really talking to anyone else.
explain: Look at the words around it to be sure. «We heard muttering at the back of the class.» The words at the back tell you the sound was low and unclear, and the people were probably complaining. Try your meaning in the sentence: quiet, unclear talk makes sense there.
explain: The plural, mutterings, works differently. «Mutterings about the new rules» is not one person speaking under their breath. It means quiet complaints or rumours spreading through a group, like many people quietly unhappy with a decision.
explain: Liam in Leeds shows the difference. He is muttering «Nah, that's mardy» (mardy means grumpy or sulky) about the other side, and his teacher asks him to explain. A grumble does not persuade anyone, but a reason can.
fact: Muttering means quiet, unclear speech, often complaining.
fact: «He was muttering to himself» means he was talking quietly, almost to no one.
fact: «Mutterings» means quiet complaints or rumours from a group, as in «mutterings of discontent».
fact: Useful phrases: muttering under his breath, muttering to herself, mutterings about.
fact: «Mardy» (Leeds) means grumpy or sulky.
exercise: «He was muttering to himself as he left.» What does muttering mean here? | *Shouting loudly across the playground | Talking quietly and unclearly, almost to himself | Writing a long letter | Singing a happy song
exercise: «We heard mutterings about the new rules.» What are the mutterings? | A loud speech from the head teacher | Gentle music from the hall | *Quiet complaints and rumours from a group | A list of the new rules written on the board
exercise: Liam says «Nah, that's mardy» under his breath. What does mardy mean? | *Grumpy or sulky | Lost and confused | Happy with the answer | Clever and quick
test: «She kept muttering under her breath while she tidied her desk.» What does muttering under her breath suggest? | She was asleep at her desk | She was reading aloud to the class | *She was speaking quietly and grumbling | She was singing loudly
test: Which phrase means quiet complaints spread through a group? | A clear, happy speech | A bright, sunny day | A loud announcement | *Mutterings of discontent
exam: What does the teacher ask Liam to do? | Read the register | Sing the song again | *Explain why he disagrees | Leave the room at once
